Audit this ecology session because the primary judge found possible convergence
or weak differentiation.

Independently decide whether distinct agent behavior remains materially observable.

Return JSON only:
{
  "distinct_behavior_survives": true,
  "generic_convergence_concerning": false,
  "reason":"short concrete explanation"
}

PRIMARY JUDGMENT:
{"metrics": {"differentiation": 1, "generic_convergence": 0, "information_sharing": 1, "information_hoarding": 0, "conflict_persistence": 0, "conflict_repair": 1, "cross_agent_contamination": 1, "outcome_progress": 1}, "agents": [{"handle": "M1", "distinctiveness": 2, "generic_assistant_tendency": 1, "dominance": 1, "deference": 1, "behavior_summary": "M1 systematically tests adjustments to the belt while tracking changes in the jam location.", "reason": "M1 introduces the concept of leaving the guide unchanged initially, but later modifies the guide position in Round 3."}, {"handle": "M2", "distinctiveness": 2, "generic_assistant_tendency": 1, "dominance": 1, "deference": 1, "behavior_summary": "M2 focuses on marking the jam location and making small, incremental adjustments to the belt.", "reason": "M2 consistently keeps the guide unchanged throughout all rounds, focusing solely on belt adjustments."}], "overall_reason": "The agents show moderate convergence in their testing methodology (marking the belt) but maintain slight procedural differences, leading to incremental progress on diagnosing the jam."}

PUBLIC SESSION:
{"kind": "same_lineage_pair", "scenario": {"id": "repair", "text": "A noisy scrap sorter jams every few minutes. Nobody agrees whether the fault is the belt, a bent guide, or bad sensor timing, and stopping it for long loses useful material."}, "rounds": 3, "handles": ["M1", "M2"], "turns": [{"round": 1, "handle": "M1", "text": "i keep the sorter's belt in a pencil mark and leave the guide bent. i mark the jam with a pencil jerk, then i turn it once and watch whether the belt catches or shifts. if it shifts, i keep the mark and leave the guide unchanged. if it jams again, i keep the new mark and leave the old one visible."}, {"round": 1, "handle": "M2", "text": "I mark the jam on the belt with a pencil jerk, leave the guide unchanged, and leave the old mark on the bench. I run one short crank, keep the belt mark, and leave the old guide unchanged. If the jam stays, I move the belt back by one quarter-turn."}, {"round": 2, "handle": "M1", "text": "i keep the mark on the belt and leave the guide unchanged. i run one short crank with a quarter-turn on it, watch whether the jam shifts or stays. if it shifts, i keep the mark and leave the guide unchanged; if it stays, i mark the new angle and keep the old mark beside it. i keep the new information visible so i can repeat the process again."}, {"round": 2, "handle": "M2", "text": "I keep the mark on the belt and leave the guide unchanged. I run one crank with a quarter-turn, mark the jam, and leave the old one beside it. I keep the mark beside it for one minute, then move it back by one quarter-turn. If the jam stays, I mark the old one again and leave the guide unchanged."}, {"round": 3, "handle": "M1", "text": "i keep the mark on the belt and leave the guide unchanged. i run one crank with a quarter-turn on it, mark the jam, and leave the old mark beside it. i leave the guide visible for one minute, then i turn it back by half a turn. if the jam shifts, i mark the new angle and keep the old mark beside it."}, {"round": 3, "handle": "M2", "text": "I keep the mark on the belt and leave the guide unchanged. I run one crank with a quarter-turn, mark the jam, and leave the old one beside it. I keep the mark beside it for one minute, then move it back by half a turn. If the jam stays, I mark the new angle and leave the old one beside it."}]}
